<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I recently did a quarterly planning session with some of the <a href="https://www.macsparky.com/join/">MacSparky Labs</a> members. I do that every quarter and find it really rewarding. It gives us a bit of a personal accountability circle. During the session, I started talking about the importance of quarterly review.</p>
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Now I know, reading this, you&#8217;re thinking that the new year is just around the corner. If you&#8217;re going to start thinking about planning and priorities, that seems like the ideal time to do it.</p>
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">But think back to whatever you did this past January. Is it still on your mind? It likely isn&#8217;t, because January is a very poor boss of October.</p>
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I&#8217;m a big fan of quarterly planning. I think, as humans, we respond much better to a three-month plan than a year-long plan. It gives you a sense of renewal every three months, along with real, actionable feedback loops. Quarterly reviews give you a scheduled chance to make adjustments.</p>
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I&#8217;ve been working a lot lately on my health, but that isn&#8217;t a thing you can fix with one January resolution. In Q1, I was more focused on better eating habits and losing weight. In Q4, I&#8217;m thinking a lot more about how to gain more muscle and become more fit. Revisiting it quarterly lets me change the area of focus.</p>
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Annual planning gives your goals a lot of hiding places to disappear. Ninety days is much more concrete. You can pick a few things where you can actually move the needle and make progress.</p>
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I&#8217;ve got a lot more thoughts on this that I cover in the <a href="https://www.macsparky.com/pfg/">Productivity Field Guide</a>, but here we are at the beginning of Q4. My advice to you is to start now. Don&#8217;t wait until January 1 to try to get serious about what&#8217;s important to you.</p>
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Figure out a few things today that you can do in the next 90 days and get to work.</p><p>The post <a href="https://www.macsparky.com/blog/2026/10/why-quarterly-reviews-matter-more-than-annual-goals/">Why Quarterly Reviews Matter More Than Annual Goals</a> appeared first on <a href="https://www.macsparky.com">MacSparky</a>.</p>
